    {
      "id": 4,
      "kind": "mother_usage",
      "payload": "{\"model_calls\":1,\"phase\":\"preparation\",\"plan\":{\"A\":\"Research a low-overhead serial O(N^2) replacement for the serpentine traversal. Store the matrix contiguously and initialize N column sums over the first S rows. For each window-row position, scan those column sums with a width-S rolling sum; advance downward by subtracting the outgoing matrix row and adding the incoming row. Use 64-bit accumulators and initialize the maximum from an actual window. Initially retain the existing input method to isolate the algorithmic gain. Measure the candidate under the unchanged harness and report its time, correctness, memory footprint, and integration-ready changes.\",\"B\":\"Research input throughput and fused streaming computation. Replace mixed cin/scanf input with one buffered fread-based signed-integer reader for both the header and matrix. Support valid whitespace, signs, INT_MIN, buffer-boundary tokens, and EOF without a final newline; do not assume seekable input. Maintain 64-bit column sums and an S-row ring of original integers, subtracting an outgoing value before overwriting its slot. Once S rows are available, compute that row of window sums with a horizontal rolling scan. This gives O(N^2) work and O(NS+N) storage without retaining the full matrix. Measure parser-only improvement separately from the fused algorithm where budget permits. Export the parser independently so G can use it with nonstreaming candidates.\",\"C\":\"Research whether ARM64 locality, vectorization, and up to four workers improve the O(N^2) computation after input costs. With a contiguous immutable matrix, partition the N-S+1 window-top rows into independent contiguous bands. Each worker initializes private 64-bit column sums for its first position, scans its band using rolling horizontal sums and contiguous entering/leaving-row updates, then contributes a local maximum. Account for repeated O(PNS) initialization, bandwidth, and worker startup; compare against the serial candidate rather than assuming parallelism wins. Use only threading supported by the unchanged build. If unavailable, focus on SIMD-friendly independent column updates and cache behavior. Avoid architecture-incompatible intrinsics and retain a correct serial fallback.\",\"G\":\"Integrate only programs produced by A/B/C, keeping a measured standalone winner available. First compare their valid standalone results, then evaluate compatible combinations such as B's parser with A's contiguous kernel or C's row-band kernel. Streaming storage and whole-matrix parallel traversal are alternative designs, not automatically composable. Measure every integrated finalist with the unchanged oracle, tests, compiler, target, and budgets; gains are not additive. Favor the best reproducible end-to-end result over isolated kernel improvements. Audit widening before subtraction, negative-only matrices, S=1, S=N-1, ring lifetimes, band boundaries, allocation sizes, and exact single-line output. Any dispatch must reflect general algorithmic costs, never test identities or expected answers.\",\"analysis\":\"The supplied source implements Problem B, maximum-sum fixed-size submatrices; A/B/C are research roles, not the other contest problems. Its main computational weakness is repeated length-S, strided column traversal, giving O(S(N-S+1)^2) work. O(N^2) rolling formulations can remove that factor, after which per-integer scanf and memory traffic may dominate. The only measured evidence is a valid baseline on four correctness cases, with mean 0.125845 seconds and samples approximately 0.1231–0.1313 seconds; there are no input dimensions or profiles establishing a bottleneck or predicted speedup. Research should therefore separate algorithm, input, and parallelism effects on the four-core ARM64 CPU. Use exact 64-bit intermediate sums: a 32-bit final maximum does not guarantee that partial or negative window sums fit 32 bits. Every candidate must remain correct for every valid input.\"},\"request_sha256\":\"df18aa57a2931e6bb0f83046e841bd258cd9fdab298b9e5e3da0700f002536be\",\"response\":{\"data\":{\"A\":\"Research a low-overhead serial O(N^2) replacement for the serpentine traversal. Store the matrix contiguously and initialize N column sums over the first S rows. For each window-row position, scan those column sums with a width-S rolling sum; advance downward by subtracting the outgoing matrix row and adding the incoming row. Use 64-bit accumulators and initialize the maximum from an actual window. Initially retain the existing input method to isolate the algorithmic gain. Measure the candidate under the unchanged harness and report its time, correctness, memory footprint, and integration-ready changes.\",\"B\":\"Research input throughput and fused streaming computation. Replace mixed cin/scanf input with one buffered fread-based signed-integer reader for both the header and matrix. Support valid whitespace, signs, INT_MIN, buffer-boundary tokens, and EOF without a final newline; do not assume seekable input. Maintain 64-bit column sums and an S-row ring of original integers, subtracting an outgoing value before overwriting its slot. Once S rows are available, compute that row of window sums with a horizontal rolling scan. This gives O(N^2) work and O(NS+N) storage without retaining the full matrix. Measure parser-only improvement separately from the fused algorithm where budget permits. Export the parser independently so G can use it with nonstreaming candidates.\",\"C\":\"Research whether ARM64 locality, vectorization, and up to four workers improve the O(N^2) computation after input costs. With a contiguous immutable matrix, partition the N-S+1 window-top rows into independent contiguous bands. Each worker initializes private 64-bit column sums for its first position, scans its band using rolling horizontal sums and contiguous entering/leaving-row updates, then contributes a local maximum. Account for repeated O(PNS) initialization, bandwidth, and worker startup; compare against the serial candidate rather than assuming parallelism wins. Use only threading supported by the unchanged build. If unavailable, focus on SIMD-friendly independent column updates and cache behavior. Avoid architecture-incompatible intrinsics and retain a correct serial fallback.\",\"G\":\"Integrate only programs produced by A/B/C, keeping a measured standalone winner available. First compare their valid standalone results, then evaluate compatible combinations such as B's parser with A's contiguous kernel or C's row-band kernel. Streaming storage and whole-matrix parallel traversal are alternative designs, not automatically composable. Measure every integrated finalist with the unchanged oracle, tests, compiler, target, and budgets; gains are not additive. Favor the best reproducible end-to-end result over isolated kernel improvements. Audit widening before subtraction, negative-only matrices, S=1, S=N-1, ring lifetimes, band boundaries, allocation sizes, and exact single-line output. Any dispatch must reflect general algorithmic costs, never test identities or expected answers.\",\"analysis\":\"The supplied source implements Problem B, maximum-sum fixed-size submatrices; A/B/C are research roles, not the other contest problems. Its main computational weakness is repeated length-S, strided column traversal, giving O(S(N-S+1)^2) work. O(N^2) rolling formulations can remove that factor, after which per-integer scanf and memory traffic may dominate. The only measured evidence is a valid baseline on four correctness cases, with mean 0.125845 seconds and samples approximately 0.1231–0.1313 seconds; there are no input dimensions or profiles establishing a bottleneck or predicted speedup. Research should therefore separate algorithm, input, and parallelism effects on the four-core ARM64 CPU. Use exact 64-bit intermediate sums: a 32-bit final maximum does not guarantee that partial or negative window sums fit 32 bits. Every candidate must remain correct for every valid input.\"},\"error\":null,\"infrastructure_error\":false,\"model\":\"gpt-6.1-sol\",\"nonce\":\"40488f12edee4f449d9319c2d7566965\",\"protocol\":\"hpc-forest-codex-mailbox-v1\",\"reasoning_effort\":\"max\",\"request_sha256\":\"df18aa57a2931e6bb0f83046e841bd258cd9fdab298b9e5e3da0700f002536be\",\"session_id\":\"665743a02b824021a697b6a4884a667c\",\"usage\":{\"cached_tokens_included_in_input\":true,\"codex_jsonl_sha256\":\"1aef8344330f90ad4dc5dd1d8d9bd84fae2975a9f7f38d74544d55b44246f6b3\",\"events\":[{\"cache_write_input_tokens\":0,\"cached_input_tokens\":0,\"input_tokens\":10214,\"output_tokens\":5449,\"reasoning_output_tokens\":4660}],\"llm_calls\":1,\"model_requested\":\"gpt-6.1-sol\",\"reasoning_effort_requested\":\"max\",\"token_accounting\":\"exact\",\"tokens\":15663,\"tool_events\":[]}},\"status\":\"complete\"}",
