      "id": 17,
      "kind": "mother_usage",
      "payload": "{\"model_calls\":1,\"phase\":\"synthesis\",\"plan\":{\"A\":\"Prioritize input conversion and streaming locality, starting from d6929c5913cf4fa6. Replace mixed cin/scanf input with one buffered fread-based integer scanner covering the header and matrix. Preserve arbitrary whitespace, optional signs, the full signed-int range, tokens crossing buffer boundaries, and EOF without a final newline. Measure the parser change independently before attempting fusion with column updates and history replacement. Retain the O(N²) streaming algorithm and N*min(S,N-S) history allocation. Use 64-bit sums and widen operands before subtraction; do not assume fixed-width or nonnegative matrix values.\",\"B\":\"Start from 0872145f07864e21 and focus on arithmetic kernels, initially leaving parsing unchanged. Preserve its balanced eight-delta NEON scan, independent maximum accumulators, type-correct int64_t storage, and scalar fallback/tails. Isolate the maintained first-window sum from C's 69c407ed932840e9: update it during entering/leaving-row processing instead of summing S columns again for every candidate row. Compare this change separately, then investigate scan-block sizing or unrolling only if measurements justify it. Vectorization assertions belong only on genuinely independent loops. Export a reusable horizontal-maximum kernel for G.\",\"C\":\"Explore a distinct horizontal-first streaming formulation rather than assuming the existing row-band variants provide parallel gains. For each input row, compute M=N-S+1 width-S sums in 64-bit arithmetic. Maintain vertical totals for those M positions, adding incoming filtered values and subtracting expiring ones; after S rows, reduce their maximum. Retain only filtered rows that will expire, using min(S,N-S)*M 64-bit history entries. This makes vertical updates and maxima independent SIMD lanes, but moves horizontal scanning to every input row and changes memory traffic, so compare end-to-end against existing programs. Keep the existing coarse row-band implementation as a separate contender only if the unchanged build actually enables OpenMP; cap it at the four available cores and preserve a serial path.\",\"G\":\"Keep 0872145f07864e21 as the measured control and fallback. First evaluate the direct integration of A's existing streaming ring with B's NEON horizontal kernel. Separately evaluate A's buffered parser on the best existing backend, then combine it with a winning streaming/SIMD integration. Isolate C's maintained first-window sum as another independent addition. Treat C's horizontal-first formulation and full-matrix threaded bands as alternative backends, not mandatory layers: concurrent row-band readers cannot safely consume A's overwritten ring. Use the unchanged evaluation and budgets to compare complete programs against their constituents. Retain a standalone child if integration regresses, and never add component speedups or declare sub-percent differences decisive without supporting measurements.\",\"analysis\":\"Only Problem B matches the supplied source. The baseline is 0.125845 s; admissible O(N²) programs range from 0.054076 to 0.055941 s. B's NEON program is provisionally fastest, approximately 2.33× baseline, but A's streaming ring is only 0.62% slower and C's simpler bands only 0.96% slower. Candidate variability is unavailable, so these rankings are not conclusive. Every candidate still uses scanf per matrix element, making input conversion a strong untested bottleneck hypothesis. ARM64 supports the NEON direction; guarded OpenMP code alone does not establish that threading was active. Preserve 64-bit intermediates and maxima initialized from actual windows for all valid inputs.\"},\"request_sha256\":\"8fa3a642274cbddb6d24243cac21970bb69849672418bb1f8aba41ae51ac69c6\",\"response\":{\"data\":{\"A\":\"Prioritize input conversion and streaming locality, starting from d6929c5913cf4fa6. Replace mixed cin/scanf input with one buffered fread-based integer scanner covering the header and matrix. Preserve arbitrary whitespace, optional signs, the full signed-int range, tokens crossing buffer boundaries, and EOF without a final newline. Measure the parser change independently before attempting fusion with column updates and history replacement. Retain the O(N²) streaming algorithm and N*min(S,N-S) history allocation. Use 64-bit sums and widen operands before subtraction; do not assume fixed-width or nonnegative matrix values.\",\"B\":\"Start from 0872145f07864e21 and focus on arithmetic kernels, initially leaving parsing unchanged. Preserve its balanced eight-delta NEON scan, independent maximum accumulators, type-correct int64_t storage, and scalar fallback/tails. Isolate the maintained first-window sum from C's 69c407ed932840e9: update it during entering/leaving-row processing instead of summing S columns again for every candidate row. Compare this change separately, then investigate scan-block sizing or unrolling only if measurements justify it. Vectorization assertions belong only on genuinely independent loops. Export a reusable horizontal-maximum kernel for G.\",\"C\":\"Explore a distinct horizontal-first streaming formulation rather than assuming the existing row-band variants provide parallel gains. For each input row, compute M=N-S+1 width-S sums in 64-bit arithmetic. Maintain vertical totals for those M positions, adding incoming filtered values and subtracting expiring ones; after S rows, reduce their maximum. Retain only filtered rows that will expire, using min(S,N-S)*M 64-bit history entries. This makes vertical updates and maxima independent SIMD lanes, but moves horizontal scanning to every input row and changes memory traffic, so compare end-to-end against existing programs. Keep the existing coarse row-band implementation as a separate contender only if the unchanged build actually enables OpenMP; cap it at the four available cores and preserve a serial path.\",\"G\":\"Keep 0872145f07864e21 as the measured control and fallback. First evaluate the direct integration of A's existing streaming ring with B's NEON horizontal kernel. Separately evaluate A's buffered parser on the best existing backend, then combine it with a winning streaming/SIMD integration. Isolate C's maintained first-window sum as another independent addition. Treat C's horizontal-first formulation and full-matrix threaded bands as alternative backends, not mandatory layers: concurrent row-band readers cannot safely consume A's overwritten ring. Use the unchanged evaluation and budgets to compare complete programs against their constituents. Retain a standalone child if integration regresses, and never add component speedups or declare sub-percent differences decisive without supporting measurements.\",\"analysis\":\"Only Problem B matches the supplied source. The baseline is 0.125845 s; admissible O(N²) programs range from 0.054076 to 0.055941 s. B's NEON program is provisionally fastest, approximately 2.33× baseline, but A's streaming ring is only 0.62% slower and C's simpler bands only 0.96% slower. Candidate variability is unavailable, so these rankings are not conclusive. Every candidate still uses scanf per matrix element, making input conversion a strong untested bottleneck hypothesis. ARM64 supports the NEON direction; guarded OpenMP code alone does not establish that threading was active. Preserve 64-bit intermediates and maxima initialized from actual windows for all valid inputs.\"},\"error\":null,\"infrastructure_error\":false,\"model\":\"gpt-6.1-sol\",\"nonce\":\"1ceaecb4b06b46febe8ba7fb6dd5ba99\",\"protocol\":\"hpc-forest-codex-mailbox-v1\",\"reasoning_effort\":\"max\",\"request_sha256\":\"8fa3a642274cbddb6d24243cac21970bb69849672418bb1f8aba41ae51ac69c6\",\"session_id\":\"665743a02b824021a697b6a4884a667c\",\"usage\":{\"cached_tokens_included_in_input\":true,\"codex_jsonl_sha256\":\"683fb34fe1deff1a5b35ac523547856a90e8f3875e536d6ac4d756c5b858b10f\",\"events\":[{\"cache_write_input_tokens\":0,\"